  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The international comparison functions as a qualitative typology of institutional readiness rather than a direct empirical ranking.",
      "what": "This study synthesizes 87 publications from 2015 to 2025 using a PRISMA-guided review alongside institutional comparisons of India, Singapore, and Canada to evaluate AI applications in land valuation and spatial planning. While machine-learning Automated Valuation Models (AVMs) consistently outperform standard hedonic regression when supplied with dense geospatial data, few studies apply AI directly, and empirical proof that these models improve statutory transparency, sustainability, or equity remains sparse. Analysis across AVM implementations in the US, EU, UK, and China demonstrates that unmitigated models routinely perpetuate assessment regressivity and appraisal bias.",
      "why": "It challenges the assumption that deploying AI in real estate valuation naturally leads to fairer or more sustainable cities, demonstrating that algorithmic precision does not translate into equitable planning without upfront data auditing and human oversight."
    }
  },
